/Contents (Artificial Intelligence \046 Machine Learning \055 Module 1\072 Foundations \046 Paradigms\012\0121\056 Artificial Intelligence vs Machine Learning vs Deep Learning\072\012Artificial Intelligence \050AI\051 refers to computer algorithms capable of executing tasks that typically demand human cognition\054 such as reasoning\054 visual perception\054 and natural language understanding\056 Machine Learning \050ML\051 is a subset of AI that uses statistical techniques to learn patterns directly from empirical datasets without explicit procedural programming\056 Deep Learning is an ML subfield based on multi\055layered Artificial Neural Networks \050ANNs\051\056\012\0122\056 Supervised and Unsupervised Learning Paradigms\072\012Supervised Learning algorithms train on labeled datasets where each input vector aligns with a target output label\056 Common tasks include classification \050e\056g\056\054 spam detection\051 and regression \050e\056g\056\054 house price forecasting\051\056 Unsupervised Learning operates on unlabeled data to identify latent patterns\054 groupings\054 or structural relationships without human guidance\056 Techniques include K\055Means Clustering\054 Hierarchical Clustering\054 and Principal Component Analysis \050PCA\051 for dimensionality reduction\056\012\0123\056 Reinforcement Learning\072\012Reinforcement Learning \050RL\051 trains an autonomous software agent inside an interactive environment using trial\055and\055error mechanisms\056 The agent observes the current state\054 selects an action\054 and receives feedback in the form of a scalar reward or penalty\056 The goal is to learn an optimal policy function that maximizes cumulative long\055term rewards over time\056 Applications include game playing \050e\056g\056\054 AlphaGo\051\054 robotics\054 and automated financial trading\056)
